<article class="env-definition" id="07HM">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/07HM">Definition <span data-tag="07HM">60.5.2</span>.</a> In Situation <a href="/tag/07MD" data-tag="07MD">60.5.1</a>. </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
A <em>divided power thickening</em> of $C$ over $(A, I, \gamma )$ is a homomorphism of divided power algebras $(A, I, \gamma ) \to (B, J, \delta )$ such that $p$ is nilpotent in $B$ and a ring map $C \to B/J$ such that 
</p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  \xymatrix{ B \ar[r] &  B/J \\  &  C \ar[u] \\  A \ar[uu] \ar[r] &  A/I \ar[u] }  \]
</div>
<p>
 is commutative. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
A <em>homomorphism of divided power thickenings</em> 
</p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  (B, J, \delta , C \to B/J) \longrightarrow (B', J', \delta ', C \to B'/J')  \]
</div>
<p>
 is a homomorphism $\varphi : B \to B'$ of divided power $A$-algebras such that $C \to B/J \to B'/J'$ is the given map $C \to B'/J'$. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
We denote $\text{CRIS}(C/A, I, \gamma )$ or simply $\text{CRIS}(C/A)$ the category of divided power thickenings of $C$ over $(A, I, \gamma )$. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
We denote $\text{Cris}(C/A, I, \gamma )$ or simply $\text{Cris}(C/A)$ the full subcategory consisting of $(B, J, \delta , C \to B/J)$ such that $C \to B/J$ is an isomorphism. We often denote such an object $(B \to C, \delta )$ with $J = \mathop{\mathrm{Ker}}(B \to C)$ being understood. 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
</article>